REL-REF    (I,,C.Weiss+,J,NIM/A,799,90,2015)
            Description of the facility
           (I,,R.Plag+,J,NIM/A,496,425,2003)
            Description of the C6D6 detector
           (I,,L.Consentino+,J,RSI,86,073509,2015)
            Description of the SiMoN2 detector
           (N,23141001,A.Kimura+,J,NST,49,708,2012)
            Another measurement from the same batch Cm material
           (N,23741001,S.Kawase+,J,NST,58,764,2021)
            Another measurement from the same batch Cm material
EXP-YEAR   (2017) May to September 2017
FACILITY   (SYNCY,2ZZZCER) CERN nTOF facility EAR2.
           Protons (7E+12 protons/pulse, pulse width of 7 nsec)
            were accelerated to 20 GeV/c with repetition rate of
            0.83 Hz at maximum.
INC-SOURCE (SPALL) Neutrons were produced in a cylindrical Pb
            spallation target (40 cm in length, 60 cm in diameter)
           Neutrons were moderated by surrounding water layer.
SAMPLE     The samples used in the experiment were acquired by
            the Japan Atomic Energy Agency (JAEA) from the
            Russian Research Institute of Atomic Research in 2007.
           The sample consists of one Cm oxide pellet with
            dimensions of 2.5 mm in radius and 0.5 mm in height,
            enclosed in an aluminum casing.
           Samples from the same batch material were used in
            previous measurements by Kimura (2012) and Kawase
            (2021).
           This sample consists of one pellet and contains
            approximately 1.1 mg of Cm-246 and 0.2 mg of Cm-248.
           The absolute masses of the different isotopes are not
            precisely known; however, the isotopic mole fractions
            at the time of the measurement (summer 2017) were
            determined and are presented in the table below.
           These values were derived from the isotopic evolution,
            taking into account concentrations measured in 2010
            by Kimura (2012) and radioactive decay.
           Isotopic abundances in percent during the Cm nTOF
            measurement campaign in August 2017:
           (94-PU-240,ENR=0.092)  9.2 +/- 0.2%
           (95-AM-243,ENR=0.012)  1.2 +/- 0.2%
           (96-CM-244,ENR=0.201) 20.1 +/- 0.4%
           (96-CM-245,ENR=0.010)  1.0 +/- 0.3%
           (96-CM-246,ENR=0.570) 57.0 +/- 1.2%
           (96-CM-247,ENR=0.028)  2.8 +/- 0.4%
           (96-CM-248,ENR=0.087)  8.7 +/- 0.2%
DETECTOR   (SCIN) Array of three C6D6 detectors horizontally with
            respect to neutron beam direction at 5 cm from the
            sample.
           The efficiency is different for each isotope, the
            values for each efficiency divided by the one of
            Pu-240 are:
           . Pu-240=1,
           . Am-243=0.938,
           . Cm-244=1.02883,
           . Cm-245=0.914994,
           . Cm-246=0.952975,
           . Cm-247=0.914994,
           . Cm-248=0.914994.
           (SI) The neutron flux was measured during the
            experiment by SiMoN2 6Li(n,t)
METHOD     (TOF) Flight path length of 19.425+/-0.001 m.
           (PHWT)
ANALYSIS   The measurements were normalized to the first
            resonance of Pu-240 at 1.056 eV using the JEFF-3.3
            library. This isotope is also present in the sample.
           To perform this normalization, the sample masses were
            adjusted to match this Pu-240 resonance.
CORRECTION Backgrounds subtracted:
           - Dummy sample (Al canning)
           - No sample
           - Fission assuming a constant efficiency to detect the
             fission events
           - Activity of the sample
COMMENT    *Victor Alcayne Aicua (2026-01-14):
